# W0012: function, method, class, enum, interface or constant that no live code uses

A function, method, class, enum, interface or constant that no live code uses is a warning, and the program still builds. Live code is `main`, each function, method and constant whose name starts with `_`, and all the code that live code calls or names. A function or a method is used when live code calls it. A class or an enum is used when live code builds it, or one of its variants. A type that names a class does not use it. A constant is used when live code or a live constant names it. An interface is used when live code names it in a bound, or in a bound of the type parameters of a live `extend` block, or calls a method that meets one of its requirements, or when a used interface names it, as an inherited interface, in the bound of a requirement or in the bound of one of its own type parameters. A conformance alone does not use an interface, because it dispatches nothing. A use in dead code does not count, and the warning shows up to three such uses. An `extend` block is live when one of its methods is live, or when it conforms a class or an enum that live code builds. A dead `extend` block of a class or an enum that live code builds gets a warning that names the block: delete it. Its methods get no warning of their own. A block has no name, so `_` cannot keep it; name one of its methods with `_` to keep the block. A method of a class or an enum that nothing builds gets no warning of its own: its class or enum gets the warning. A dead method of a live block gets its own warning. A name that starts with `_` gets no warning. The warning does not show while the program has an error, because a use in code that fails to check can be missed. There is no fix, because only you know if the code is still necessary.

## Why

Dead code is often what a refactor leaves behind. It costs time to read and to keep up to date, and it can make a reader think that a path still runs. Only a use in live code counts. When `old_report` is the only function that calls `format_row`, and nothing calls `old_report`, both are dead. Each one gets its own warning, and the warning for `format_row` shows the call in `old_report`. Delete both, or name `old_report` `_old_report` to keep it on purpose: then `format_row` is live too.

## Broken

```sisu
fn old_report() {
    print("report")
}

fn main() {
    print("done")
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn main() {
    print("done")
}
```

## An `extend` block

The block of `Crate` below has two methods, and nothing calls them. `Crate` is built, so the warning names the block, and the two methods get none. The interface `Shape` is used only in the bound of the block, so it gets a warning too. The label shows the bound once, not once for each method. Delete the block, and then `Shape`.

```sisu
interface Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Crate<T> {
    item: T
}

extend<T: Shape> Crate<T> {
    fn one(self) -> f64 {
        self.item.area()
    }

    fn two(self) -> f64 {
        self.item.area() * 2.0
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Crate(item: 1)
    print("{c.item}")
}
```

